1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  MEMOIRS OF OLE WINDINGLAND
2 CONT
2 CONT   From the Account of
2 CONT
2 CONT   Olga Windingland
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Compiled 1958-1959)
2 CONT
2 CONT Translated from the Narwegian by            i
2 CONT
2 CONT  Bergetta Christopherson
2 CONT
2 CONT    February 21, 1974
2 CONT
2 CONT     Typed by
2 CONT
2 CONT    Louise Lovelady
2 CONT
2 CONT    (grandaughter)
2 CONT
2 CONT    July 1975
2 CONT
2 CONT A little about Ole Windingland's background and youth:
2 CONT
2 CONT Die's father, TBnner, was born on Gaard Windingland in Sta-
2 CONT
2 CONT vanger ()ounty, or District, in the year 1833. His father'r name
2 CONT
2 CONT was Askel Windingland, and his nother died when Tdnnes was a lit-
2 CONT
2 CONT tle boy.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Tdnnes, Ole's father, remembered there was a fire in the house
2 CONT
2 CONT at Yindingland, and his father, Rrkel, carried him out; bur many of
2 CONT
2 CONT the sisters and brothers died in the fire. Only T~nner and his
2 CONT
2 CONT older sister, Targeir, were saved from the fire. Arkel himrelf ia-
2 CONT
2 CONT ter died from the burn injuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Little Tdnner grew up. His sirter, Targeir, war married and
2 CONT
2 CONT moved to Gaard Sundvor. Now Tpnnes had the birthright to the farm
2 CONT
2 CONT and Her entitled to buy out Targeir. But when Tdnner was young he
2 CONT
2 CONT got a defective leg--probably from polio--and it became difficult
2 CONT
2 CONT to work The farm, so Windingiand was sold out of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT   TBnner was married to Lire Larzdatter Helland from Bjerkreim
2 CONT
2 CONT sogn. She was a good wife and brought up a nice family of seven
2 CONT
2 CONT children. The first was Rnderr, later called Fabo [Norwegian for
2 CONT
2 CONT "Father's brother"]. born November 23, 1865. (Died February 1, 1912--
2 CONT
2 CONT not marriedi. The second child was Malena, who married a man named
2 CONT
2 CONT Opsal, and their daughter war irther Friestad. The third child was
2 CONT
2 CONT Abigail, married to Eliassen. Their children were Eriing, Lindis,
2 CONT
2 CONT Oivind, and Morsa. These two sisters died at an early age. The
2 CONT
2 CONT fourth child of Lire and Tbnnes was Lars, barn the 1Sth of May, 1872.
2 CONT
2 CONT Larr and Ole have shared both the good and difficult through the
2 CONT
2 CONT years. from childhood and through the years life's destiny seemed
2 CONT
2 CONT to lead them together, and they became neighbors here in America.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Ole war the fifth child of Lire and Tbnnes Windingiand. He
2 CONT
2 CONT war born the fourth of January , 1815, an the farm Store Ege, where
2 CONT
2 CONT they lived for some time. The sixth child was Ingeborg, who died
2 CONT
2 CONT at the age of nine years. Ole remembered coming home from school
2 CONT
2 CONT and ingeborg war dead. She had had running ears and bad headacher
2 CONT
2 CONT with restiesiness and night before. The seventh child wat Karina,
2 CONT
2 CONT the rmailest in the fami ly, born the twenty-fourth of March, 1880.
2 CONT
2 CONT Karina came into the same community in America as Lars and Ole in
2 CONT
2 CONT 1903. She war married to Kornell Christopherson in 1906.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Lise and TBnnes bought a house in Egersund and operated a
2 CONT
2 CONT livery accommodation for travellers as the need arose. Besides
2 CONT
2 CONT being the town's taxi-man, he bought hider from individuals and
2 CONT
2 CONT resold them to large cooipaniei. They also had ronie small piecer
2 CONT
2 CONT of land outside of town. Timer were difficult in Norway at this
2 CONT
2 CONT time, since Norway and Sweden were rtill under the same King.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Ole and Larr had many epirodes to relate about their chiid-
2 CONT
2 CONT hood. TBnnes' livery service war of ten shifted over on the boys,
2 CONT
2 CONT so they were called on to sit on the wagons and drive at night in
2 CONT
2 CONT the winter time, resulting in frostbitten feet. Often the home
2 CONT
2 CONT journey became very tiresome because the horrer were to drive lei-
2 CONT
2 CONT rurely coming back from these trips. Mother Lire would come and
2 CONT
2 CONT befriend the tired houses and send the boyr in the house where she
2 CONT
2 CONT would warn The small feet as best the could. "Blessed by the mem-
2 CONT
2 CONT ory of Motherl ihe best mother in the world:" said the boys. (Yel-
2 CONT
2 CONT signet vere Mors minde. Oen beste Mor i verden.) After such driv-
2 CONT
2 CONT ing experiences, there was no fun in going to school and the les-
2 CONT
2 CONT sons were often neglected. Oie thought, "That can go the way it
2 CONT
2 CONT wiill" (Det fik det gas rom det kunde med:) He liked better to
2 CONT
2 CONT do worthwhile wort.
2 CONT
2 CONT  The brothers and risters grew up and roon they were attracted
2 CONT
2 CONT to go out into the world to explore what it had to offer them.
2 CONT
2 CONT  When Ole war 17 or 18 years old he began dreaming about Amer-
2 CONT
2 CONT ica. R cousin of Ole, Tollef Johnron, was home from America for
2 CONT
2 CONT a visit and war preparing to return. Listening to Toilef'r storier
2 CONT
2 CONT of America put ideas in Ole'i youthful adventuresome mind. He con-
2 CONT
2 CONT tracted what we called in Norway. "America FevOrl" His father
2 CONT
2 CONT din't like this and said he didn't have any money to take that
2 CONT
2 CONT trip and without money Oie couldn't get fari A brother of Tallef
2 CONT
2 CONT Johnson, idnnes Malmoy, who had grown up on Gaard Malmoy and too~
2 CONT
2 CONT his name from the farm, also came from Rrrerica for a visit. Oie
2 CONT
2 CONT got the Rmerica Fever worre than ever, but he didn't get a chance
2 CONT
2 CONT to go then either.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Ole tells about several brothers born on Sundvor who were
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole's cousins. One, Rnders, was a rchool teacher with beautiful
2 CONT
2 CONT handwriting. He wrote, "En erindring til Ole i et Ny Tertamente."
2 CONT
2 CONT (This New Testament is a remembrance to Oie) and gave i t to ~le
2 CONT
2 CONT when he was a young boy. (We call this "skjonskrift" in Norway.)
2 CONT
2 CONT rhir Testament he used ail his life through. Another brother of
2 CONT
2 CONT nnderr was Hanz who came to America and became a neighbor, which
2 CONT
2 CONT hecame a continuing relationship of Sundvor and Windingland. Ano-
2 CONT
2 CONT ther brother of Anders and Hanr Sundvor did not call himself after
2 CONT
2 CONT the Sundvor Gaard name but instead took the Johnson name. Thir is
2 CONT
2 CONT the before-mentioned Tollef Johnson who had returned to visit Nor-
2 CONT
2 CONT way and drove Ole's aspirations to get to America: He retried on
2 CONT
2 CONT nice farm on the Grafton prairie, which became Ole'r first place to
2 CONT
2 CONT rtay as a newcomer. The family and farm of the Johnrons became
2 CONT
2 CONT dear to Ole. iven after 10 years from the date of his coming to
2 CONT
2 CONT America, he loved to talk about that event. "it warms my heart to
2 CONT
2 CONT come to this place again and talk to the johnson family.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Now to tell about ~ie'r first trip to America in 1893. While
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole's strong desire to go to America grew, one Carl john Iverren
2 CONT
2 CONT heard of this and said to Oie, "My son Emil is a seaman and saves
2 CONT
2 CONT money and sends home for me to put in the bank. If your father will
2 CONT
2 CONT give security for a loan for the ticket to America then you could
2 CONT
2 CONT get money to go." Then the father couldn't say no and he wrote a
2 CONT
2 CONT guarantee for a loan of $65.00 (American money), enough for the ti-
2 CONT
2 CONT cket to the U.S. He also got 20 kroner (about ~5.00) for pocket
2 CONT
2 CONT money. Ole was 18 years old on january 4, 1893. On the 2ith of Feb-
2 CONT
2 CONT ruary, 1893, Ole stood ready and happy, prepared for hir journey to
2 CONT
2 CONT America. it was on a Saturday, at 4:00 in the afternoon, that the
2 CONT
2 CONT train should leave Egersund for Stavanger to reach the boat which
2 CONT
2 CONT would go to ing~and that same evening. :he youngster thought i t a
2 CONT
2 CONT great thrill to embark out into the unknown woridl Oie himself raid,
2 CONT
2 CONT "iars, who was three years older thanme, had better sense. He had
2 CONT
2 CONT to leave the table while I was eating the last meal before I left.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole'r father also had to leave the table: he surely had the same feel-
2 CONT
2 CONT ing as Lars. Mother said, "Maybe you shouldn't go since this goes
2 CONT
2 CONT so againrt father.
2 CONT
2 CONT   When Oie got to Stavanger he boarded the boar that took hini to
2 CONT
2 CONT Cngland. There he got on the ship Za*nin, which railed for the Dom-
2 CONT
2 CONT inolina Line. On the boat trip from Stavanger Ole became helplerrly
2 CONT
2 CONT seasick an the deck at night and frare his feet. This resulted in
2 CONT
2 CONT large rarer and swelling. For a long time he couldn't get shoer
2 CONT
2 CONT on. Now he couldn't turn around and go to Mother. Ole raid him-
2 CONT
2 CONT self, "He who wants to be out will have to stay out:" (Den som vii
2 CONT
2 CONT ute vere, maa ute bli:) The journey was troublesome and difficult.
2 CONT
2 CONT He wrote home from England but never mentioned anything of hir trou-
2 CONT
2 CONT bles. He said, "Seasickness ir nothing to fool with. One becomes
2 CONT
2 CONT ro helpless." At this time there was pear and almost no care for
2 CONT
2 CONT the sick passengers. The boat war very inadequate and nor like
2 CONT
2 CONT the newer, modern oner.
2 CONT
2 CONT   in England where they got on the larger boat, iannin. a travel-
2 CONT
2 CONT ling partner, Amund Svinland, sold Ole a pair of American-made over-
2 CONT
2 CONT shoes, to use on those swollen frostbitten feet. Now the rhip rtar-
2 CONT
2 CONT ted across the Atlantic Ocean, using both rail and steam power. Be-
2 CONT
2 CONT fore he bearded the boat in England he had his feet dressed by 2
2 CONT
2 CONT doctor. His feet were so sore he could scarcely walk. The Ooctar
2 CONT
2 CONT raid they were frostSitten. Ole thought he understood same English
2 CONT
2 CONT for in Norway he had heard it said, "It is so cold it biter." (Det
2 CONT
2 CONT er sea Lait at det biter.) But a greater sisunderstanding came
2 CONT
2 CONT when he got back on the boat and the doctor there told him that he
2 CONT
2 CONT should come back at eight a'clock. Ole said he thought he was so
2 CONT
2 CONT smart in English, but when the doctor had said eight o'clock Ole
2 CONT
2 CONT thought he meant "kiotLen et" (one o'clock). Ole says, "Naar
2 CONT
2 CONT trar vi forstaar for meget, de er vi regtig dumme." (When we think
2 CONT
2 CONT we understand much, then we are really dumb.) It resulted that he
2 CONT
2 CONT went no more to the doctor and go no more help for the sore feet.
2 CONT
2 CONT  After ten days out on the Atlantic the axle on the boat brake.
2 CONT
2 CONT For six dayr they struggled to repair it. After same days a boat
2 CONT
2 CONT came by and offered help which Zannia felt was too expenrive, and
2 CONT
2 CONT rignaled the boat to go on. They continued with repairs until 2
2 CONT
2 CONT "Puf, puf" gave hope it would start, but it stopped again. Some of
2 CONT
2 CONT the passengers became angry that they had refused help, but finally
2 CONT
2 CONT it started and all were happy.
2 CONT
2 CONT   They were two dayr from Halifax and there they landed. Then
2 CONT
