2 CONT acquainted with her sister-in-law to be. She well knew as she jour-
2 CONT
2 CONT neyed on to Edinburg that Lovisa would become Ole's wife. Karina
2 CONT
2 CONT stayed with Ole through the spring and summer, and what a help she
2 CONT
2 CONT was! There were preparations to be made for the wedding before
2 CONT
2 CONT Lovisa should arrive. The wedding date was August 23rd, 1903, and
2 CONT
2 CONT Karina was Lovisa's attendant.
2 CONT
2 CONT    The first home for Ole and his bride was the one-room loghouse
2 CONT
2 CONT with what we call now the wash house (vaskehuset) or bunk-house,
2 CONT
2 CONT built in 1902 which stood so close to the loghouse that a closet
2 CONT
2 CONT space was built between the two buildings thus joining them. This
2 CONT
2 CONT space was used for hanging clothes, but there was no door between,
2 CONT
2 CONT so it meant to go outside to get to the other part of the house'
2 CONT
2 CONT This was later improved with doors. (The first year it was built
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole used it for storing flax). It had been handy to have this ad-
2 CONT
2 CONT ditional well-constructed living space north of the loghouse on that
2 CONT
2 CONT wedding day in August. There were many guests. Ovnand's daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT was secured as the cook. Even though a shower of rain came that
2 CONT
2 CONT afternoon, everything was joy and happiness with shelter over their
2 CONT
2 CONT headsl The season was quite dry, but even so Ole was financially
2 CONT
2 CONT able to buy the 80 acres from Mr. Lot. The mortgage contract signed
2 CONT
2 CONT by Ole Windingland and wife, Lovisa Windingland, dated September 7,
2 CONT
2 CONT 1903, (two weeks after their marriage) states that 5500.00 was to
2 CONT
2 CONT be paid December 1, 1903, and the balance of 01,800.00 was due on
2 CONT
2 CONT or before Deceisber 1, 1307, with interest of n'% for both notes from
2 CONT
2 CONT the date until Tull payment. (Warranty deed was dated Septemher 9,
2 CONT
2 CONT 1903). All payments were met and on September 21, 1907, the land
2 CONT
2 CONT was clear of debt.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Th; winter of 1903-011 found Ole and Lovisa busy improving their
2 CONT
2 CONT home with a door into the clothes room between the two living areas--
2 CONT
2 CONT the newest part constructed in 1902. All winter they had a carpen-
2 CONT
2 CONT ter with them repairing the barn. Now there would be place for
2 CONT
2 CONT twelve horses and hayloft that would hold ten loads of hay. This
2 CONT
2 CONT building was all new and the old horse barn was used for cattle. All
2 CONT
2 CONT this was a tremendous improvement and gave him much satisfaction.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now there would also be room for two new colts that were arriving
2 CONT
2 CONT in March. The barn now and then had several additions added and
2 CONT
2 CONT was called "the old barn." What stands now as the barn was built
2 CONT
2 CONT in 1919.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ole wanted more land so in March 1904 he bought 40 acres from
2 CONT
2 CONT Knut Johnson, a neighbor, for S950.00, which sum ~ras to be paid
2 CONT
2 CONT that same year. It was a late spring and Ole got started seeding
2 CONT
2 CONT the 25th of April. The fields were pretty wet and some of the older j
2 CONT
2 CONT farmers were guessing that Ole was pretty foolish to be out when the
2 CONT
2 CONT fields weren't fit to cultivate. Well, it wasn't so easy' Ole had
2 CONT
2 CONT only six horses and two of them had colts, so he couldn't drive
2 CONT
2 CONT them too severely. He owned a quarter and three 40-acre plots, was
2 CONT
2 CONT renting a quarter from Lars, and also one from Paalsned, so that
2 CONT
2 CONT made three and three-fourths quarters that had to be worked. So he
2 CONT
2 CONT had to start early] The earliest seeding had been on the 80 and it
2 CONT
2 CONT proved to De wise, as he got 24 bushels to the acre. The rust got
2 CONT
2 CONT that that was seeded late. Many fields had to be simply burned,
2 CONT
2 CONT for there wasn't grain enough to make harvest worthwhile. That fall
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole's crop came through well and he sold wheat for $1.00 a bushel.
2 CONT
2 CONT With ambitious fervor he hauled and sold wheat, and on the 24th of
2 CONT
2 CONT September he paid for the newly-acquired 40 acres from Johnson. This
2 CONT
2 CONT tract gave him a more roomy yard, as it extended west from the road
2 CONT
2 CONT and ·east to the approximate-place where the granary now stands,
2 CONT
2 CONT and the plot called "5 acres." It was only a year ago since he had
2 CONT
2 CONT purchased the 80 from Lee, and he had to pay the interest on the
2 CONT
2 CONT $1,800.00 debt he owed him this year also.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The spring of 1904 was truly a busy one. Karina was staying
2 CONT
2 CONT with thenl,, and one day she came to Ole and said, "Ole, you must hurry
2 CONT
2 CONT to town." Well, Ole Ihouyht he could do that if it was necessary.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was true that there were many things to be made ready in the
2 CONT
2 CONT house these days as Oie and Lovisa were looking forward to a new
2 CONT
2 CONT little one in their home. And surely the time soon neared when Lov-
2 CONT
2 CONT isa gave birth to a daughter on May 29th, 1901. She was named Lisa j
2 CONT
2 CONT after her grandmother in Korway. Ole related how Karina came to
2 CONT
2 CONT him with the little bundle and there was that little round face! A
2 CONT
2 CONT joyful time for the happy parents. Now God had blessed them with
2 CONT
2 CONT all good things. Even if the house wasn't the best, life with a
2 CONT
2 CONT little child in the house gave the future new challenges.
2 CONT
2 CONT    When threshing season came, the early seeding on the recfntly-
2 CONT
2 CONT purchased 40 acres proved the best. There were often problems dur-
2 CONT
2 CONT ing threshing tinie, particularly if the season should be rainy, be-
2 CONT
2 CONT cause the farmer would have to provide food and shelter for both
2 CONT
2 CONT crew and horses while he would have to look at his rain-damaged
2 CONT
2 CONT grain out in the fields. The "rig" that Ole hired had not been
2 CONT
2 CONT paid for, but the man who had invested some money in it wanted the
2 CONT
2 CONT thrfshing outfit on his farm while the weather was good. It wasn't
2 CONT
2 CONT a good practice to leave a job that you had started and go to ano-
2 CONT
2 CONT ther. But it wasn't so easy for the nian either as he owed money
2 CONT
2 CONT on the rig, so what do you suppose he did? During the night he mo-
2 CONT
2 CONT ved the outfit without Ole's knowledge, and that was a big disap-
2 CONT
2 CONT pointment, but--. Shortly after, fall rains began, and one shower
2 CONT
2 CONT after another. The man with the rig had the problem of providing
2 CONT
2 CONT for horses and crew quite a long time before he got his threshing
2 CONT
2 CONT done. Later when the weather finally cleared, Ole got his grain
2 CONT
2 CONT threshed also. Now it was to plow and harrow in preparation For
2 CONT
2 CONT next year's crop.
2 CONT
2 CONT    In the spring of 1905, the farmers were busy in their fields
2 CONT
2 CONT again. Lars farmed his quarter and rented another quarter from Al-
2 CONT
2 CONT fred Lindell, which land had been known as the Syvert Burke land and
2 CONT
2 CONT in recent years was purchased by Lawrence and Anders. Lars contin-
2 CONT
2 CONT ued to live with Oie and they worked together that summer. However,
2 CONT
2 CONT Lars decided to go to Norway that fall, and he spent the winter
2 CONT
2 CONT there. Jorgen Ivesdal stayed at Ole's. This was the eventful win-
2 CONT
2 CONT ter when little Edith was born on January 17, 1906. She was named
2 CONT
2 CONT after her grandma, Eline Odegaard. Now Lisa had a little sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lars came back from Norway in the spring and together with Ole they
2 CONT
2 CONT farmed similarly as the last year.
2 CONT
2 CONT    In January 1907 Lars sold a 40 of land that lay cast of the
2 CONT
2 CONT 40 Ole had bought from Knut Johnson in 1904. The price was $800.
2 CONT
2 CONT This wds a convenient tract to gain as it made a complete home
2 CONT
2 CONT quarter. Lars bought three 40-acre parcels fi-om Ole Weld for whom
2 CONT
2 CONT he had worked when he first came from Norway in 1897. Now Lars
2 CONT
2 CONT was getting a living place established also: He later made a trade
2 CONT
2 CONT with Mrs. Gunhild Johnson of three 40-acre plots and along with pur-
2 CONT
2 CONT chase of another 80 acres, he acquired a complete fine farm. This
2 CONT
2 CONT was the year that he was married to Nikka Haar.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Ole relates a story which Lars enjoyed telling to tease Ole.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was in the fall of 1904. Lisa was a baby, and as Lars and Ole
2 CONT
2 CONT were out in the fields with each their binder, Lovisa, with the
2 CONT
2 CONT little girl on her arm, came to the field with lunch. She first 8
2 CONT
2 CONT came to Lars' binder and he took Lisa in his arms and Lovisa went
2 CONT
2 CONT on further to Ole to meet him with his lunch. But then Ole got
2 CONT
2 CONT thoroughly frightened and scared, "That little girl could scare the
2 CONT
2 CONT horses and we would have a runawayl" He yelled and waved his arms
2 CONT
2 CONT to try to stop them from coming toward him. Ole was trying to pro-
2 CONT
2 CONT teet the dearest ones he had. Lars was an excellent horseman and
2 CONT
2 CONT nothing serious happened, but Lars had gotten a good teasing joke
2 CONT
2 CONT (leet) on Olef
2 CONT
2 CONT     On October 9, 1907, little Margit was born. She was named
2 CONT
2 CONT after Malena, Ole's sister. A fourth little daughter, Thelma, who
2 CONT
2 CONT was named after her grandfather TBnnes, was born on October 8, 1909.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole and Lovisa are well settled (trygt paa sin tre) with two guar-
2 CONT
2 CONT ters of land paid for, and their growing sweft daughters. They are
2 CONT
2 CONT still young in years; 01E is 32 and Lovisa, 27. It was in 1907
