2 CONT     In the sunlnierr of 1308 Ole bought 3 quarter of land known as
2 CONT
2 CONT the Valdres land for 54000.00. He took a alortgage on the tract for
2 CONT
2 CONT $1,800.00 at 5", interest due in 1913. Full payment was made when          i
2 CONT
2 CONT it was due in December of that year. This farm was part of an es-
2 CONT
2 CONT tate of a man from Valdrfs, Norway, by the name of Syvert Bakken.
2 CONT
2 CONT The quarter upon which the house stands cost $4.050.00. Now Ole
2 CONT
2 CONT had three quarters to work for himself, with only horses, and the
2 CONT
2 CONT machinery was quite inferior at that time. The year 1909 found
2 CONT
2 CONT both Ole and Lars settled in each their home with their families.
2 CONT
2 CONT    On a Sunday afternoon in June the brothers were out on the
2 CONT
2 CONT Valdres land pumping water for cattle that were pastured there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lars told Ole that Nil:ka was home alone and invited Ole to come
2 CONT
2 CONT with him home. They started walking on the Gvnand land when they
2 CONT
2 CONT noticed how dark the clouds were, as though a storm might be coming.
2 CONT
2 CONT Suddenly a high wind with rain came upon them, and there was no-
2 CONT
2 CONT thing to do hut to lie down and hang on to the grass. The storm
2 CONT
2 CONT was intense and to the side of them they saw a newly-build granary
2 CONT
2 CONT of Ovnand come flying past them in the flood of water. It follo~ed
2 CONT
2 CONT the coulee in an e;jteriy dii·ection until it hit the bridge that
2 CONT
2 CONT was built north of the farnstead. The granary was smashed and
2 CONT
2 CONT spread all over the area. The heavy poles under the bridge stood
2 CONT
2 CONT firm, but water flooded on both sides so that it all had to be re-
2 CONT
2 CONT paired before travel could be resumed. Ovnand had another grain
2 CONT
2 CONT bin built on skids that blew away in that same storm, but that one
2 CONT
2 CONT got caught in the bank of the coulee and wasn't destroyed. Ole
2 CONT
2 CONT and Lars has intended to get into a granary for shelter from the
2 CONT
2 CONT storm, but this was the one that was shattered-into slivers.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Lovisa and Ole started planning on a new modern home. Andrew
2 CONT
2 CONT Lee, from whom Ole had bought land, was a carpenter, and he was hi-
2 CONT
2 CONT red to construct the house. It was a dry year with rather poor in-
2 CONT
2 CONT come from the farm. Ole thought he should have had enough money to
2 CONT
2 CONT build the house, but expenses always run higher than one Expects,
2 CONT
2 CONT so he decided to take a mortgage for $2,000.00 on his land to handle
2 CONT
2 CONT the finances. It was a long-term loan which would fall due in 1923,
2 CONT
2 CONT and could not be paid earlier---(5X interest). This got to be an
2 CONT
2 CONT exceedingly i,usy season. He hauled lumber from Edirlburgg with hor-
2 CONT
2 CONT ses. It took niany many loads since there was going to be no spariny
2 CONT
2 CONT of material. There was no contract on the project, and the carpen-
2 CONT
2 CONT ters were paid by the day. Lovisa was very busy cooking for the
2 CONT
2 CONT workers, and with four little girls it was more than she could han-
2 CONT
2 CONT dle. She could have used two girls to help. Ellen Hoy was one
2 CONT
2 CONT who helped Lovisa. Lisa was now six years and Thelma less than a' j
2 CONT
2 CONT year of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT    It was necessary to provide meat for the family, so Ole deci-
2 CONT
2 CONT ded to butcher. He was unfortunate in getting a cut on his knee
2 CONT
2 CONT in which a raging skin infection known as Flying Fire developed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The doctor said Ole would have to be in bed for two weeks, for if
2 CONT
2 CONT the infection should spread, it would be exceedingly dangerous to
2 CONT
2 CONT his life. "And me, who is so very busy! I felt like the doctor had
2 CONT
2 CONT slapped me in the facfl" Well, he stayed in bed one week and im-
2 CONT
2 CONT proved steadily, but with activity the skin started to swell again.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was at this time that Ole felt the urgency to get out of bed
2 CONT
2 CONT and go out to supervise the men with the threshing. Due to a very
2 CONT
2 CONT dry season in the summer of 1910, the crop had been mowed and raked
2 CONT
2 CONT together with a hay rake. To pick this up from the ground without
2 CONT
2 CONT also picking up rocks was difficult and particularly damaging to
2 CONT
2 CONT the threshing oiachine in case stones should get into the raked grain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of Ole's worrying with the farm work it took a longer time
2 CONT
2 CONT to be completely rid of his skin infection.
2 CONT
2 CONT     In the fall of 1910 they moved into the new spacious house.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was no heating system installed so ovens were used to keep
2 CONT
2 CONT warm. In the spring of 1911 Andfrs was born, and we can imagine
2 CONT
2 CONT what joy there was to have a little boy in the family circle with
2 CONT
2 CONT four girls. How good it \\ras to have plenty of room in the new house.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the fall plans were made to get a furnace and water system in-
2 CONT
2 CONT stalled. They got a plumber from Grand Forks to do the job. There
2 CONT
2 CONT were various expenses ~!ith the purchase of twelve radiators, ade-
2 CONT
2 CONT quate pipes, pressure system, bath fixtures, and water installations.
2 CONT
2 CONT A new.well had to be dug and with the furnace installed, several
2 CONT
2 CONT thdusand dollars' improvement was made on the house.
2 CONT
2 CONT    A message from Norway in the fall of 1911 brought news that
2 CONT
2 CONT father, T~nnes Windingland, had died. In Mother's loneliness she
2 CONT
2 CONT had written and asked if someone couldn't come home to visit. After
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole was through with spring's work in 1912, and the plunibing job
2 CONT
2 CONT was finished in the house, he took a quick trip to see his niother.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left the 29tlr of May and retiirnfd on July 29. This tiiiie 01~
2 CONT
2 CONT went first to Grorud, Norway, wliere Lovisa's nlother, Elina Odeyaard,
2 CONT
2 CONT and her five children lived (these were Lovisa's half brothers
2 CONT
2 CONT and sisters). They wanted to come to America, so they proceeded
2 CONT
2 CONT to prepare for the trip. Mrs. Odegaard wanted to visit her rela-
2 CONT
2 CONT tives. They lived on a little farm close to Hamar which was ac-
2 CONT
2 CONT cessible only by train. Ole got to meet Lovisa's grandparents also.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had prepared a suniptuous nieal, but Ole had gotten a severe
2 CONT
2 CONT headache which took away his appetite so he didn't eat anything.
2 CONT
2 CONT But he enjoyed visiting with the people as well as learning about
2 CONT
2 CONT the work and activity on the gaard.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Now Mother was waiting in Egersund, as only a mother can wait
2 CONT
2 CONT in lonely hours. O!e weeps in remfmSering of what she said when
2 CONT
2 CONT he arrived, "Nu ble du drug nu Broen." Mother was always so patient,
2 CONT
2 CONT and this was the only reproach or scolding he got.    (I3roen was an
2 CONT
2 CONT idiom used to show affection in his part of Nouway.)  This struck
2 CONT
2 CONT his heart and he was sorry that he hadn't once written to her about
2 CONT
2 CONT his work. It was a convenient time to visit Abigael while he was
2 CONT
2 CONT in Ostiann in Grorud visiting Grandma Odegaard. kbigael lived in
2 CONT
2 CONT Drobak, and was very sick with a serious blood condition (anemia,
2 CONT
2 CONT probably). It was fortunate that Ole came now as he gave such
2 CONT
2 CONT great spiritual help. She thanked him for helping her to see the
2 CONT
2 CONT grace she had in Christ. Others had told her that she was a good
2 CONT
2 CONT person, but it didn't give her peace of heart until she got to see
2 CONT
2 CONT that salvation is all of grace. Ole knew what it was like to be a
2 CONT
2 CONT seeking soul and now he could help his sick sister to look to the
2 CONT
2 CONT Savior and His righteousness which is for all who know themselves
2 CONT
2 CONT as sinners and pray for n:ercy. Abigail died a-few months later.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~aiena had died five years previously of the same ailment. Ole's
2 CONT
2 CONT mother lived until 1924.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Lovisa's mother and five half brothers and sisters accompanied
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole on the return trip. He bought boat fare in Stavanger for them
