2 CONT mony. Nina was married to Jacob Peterson and Selma niarried Gtto
2 CONT
2 CONT Olson. In 1933 Ole and Lovisa's oldest daughter, Lisa, was married,
2 CONT
2 CONT and again it was cozy and convenient to have a large house for such
2 CONT
2 CONT a festive occasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Other buildings constructed on the farm were granaries. The
2 CONT
2 CONT first was built in 1906 and anotiier like it in 1913, and ~ere joined
2 CONT
2 CONT together. A chicken house was built in 1913, and other buildings,
2 CONT
2 CONT including a smoke house and a small building in which were hung hor-
2 CONT
2 CONT ses' harnesses. This stood near the new barn but has since been
2 CONT
2 CONT removed. Yes, it took all Die's energy to plan and erect the farm-
2 CONT
2 CONT stead for him and his family.
2 CONT
2 CONT    To have sufficient water supply was a problem until the well
2 CONT
2 CONT was dug deep enough where the windmill now stands. Ole remembers
2 CONT
2 CONT that in 1915, which was a very dry fall, he was most concerned that
2 CONT
2 CONT the water supply might be exhausted when the threshing crew with
2 CONT
2 CONT horses and men would be there through threshing season. So to avoid
2 CONT
2 CONT such a dilemma, he hauled loads and loads of water from a spring
2 CONT
2 CONT at Aliiicn's and filled up his well! It wi:s a big ;iob, bui there
2 CONT
2 CONT was sui~iicient water! There hall becrl I::iiry difficult tilnes witlr
2 CONT
2 CONT insufficient \\rater in the past. One tiii;e I;e hireti a Inan 1~,110 said
2 CONT
2 CONT he could locate water, and he drilletl f0O feet in one spot ~:itil no
2 CONT
2 CONT great results, and tliis happened Ii;any tiii;es. It c·ii~sn't until 1916
2 CONT
2 CONT that they struck water successfully and theii water poured out irr
2 CONT
2 CONT abundance.
2 CONT
2 CONT     In 1959, here we sit alone with the sun shiniiig in its beauty
2 CONT
2 CONT on a February afternoon. The sutoniatic oil heating system keeps
2 CONT
2 CONT us ~!arm and contfortable. Ole in his 84th year is remembering past
2 CONT
2 CONT years that were so different froin the present. Then they had oak
2 CONT
2 CONT tree groves on the land, and soine of this was grubbed out. This had
2 CONT
2 CONT to be done before the ground froze, so vhen fall field work was
2 CONT
2 CONT done it was to haul holrre this cut wood and sort it. Some couid be
2 CONT
2 CONT used for fence posts so they were trimaed and piled. Then one pile
2 CONT
2 CONT was madff up of wood thut could be used iii the cook stove. n third
2 CONT
2 CONT heap consisted of roots infiltrated rritii dirt and stones that could-
2 CONT
2 CONT n't be sawed. When the weather was at the 20-to-3U-degree-below
2 CONT
2 CONT mark in winter, the ideal time came to sl,lit these roots to a small
2 CONT
2 CONT enough size for the furnace or heating stove. A combination of
2 CONT
2 CONT wood and coal was used for heating then. It was o~ch work of course,
2 CONT
2 CONT and is a sharp contrast to the convenierrce of today, of which the
2 CONT
2 CONT people of this generation are not aware. It required courageous
2 CONT
2 CONT spirit and arms of steel!
2 CONT
2 CONT    Ole tells a little account of a treasured sled that he had as
2 CONT
2 CONT a boy. In the town of Egersund there r~as a sledding hill near Main
2 CONT
2 CONT Street. It was a rather dangerous place. If the sleds got out of
2 CONT
2 CONT hand, tiiey might hit people. So the authorities had forbidden sled-
2 CONT
2 CONT ding here and would take the sleds.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ole had gotten a new sled and it was his pride and joy. He
2 CONT
2 CONT  loaned it to a friend, and don't you suppose he had gone off limits:
2 CONT
2 CONT  The town authority, Mr. Ryg, took the sled from the hoy. Ole was
2 CONT
2 CONT  right there, and toldRyg the sled was his, and he wanted it back.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Mr. Ryg went off with the sled and Ole followed behind saying, "I
2 CONT
2 CONT  am the son of Tlnncs Windingland." ~ut that didn't help.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ole had a sharp knife in his pocket and he imagined that he
2 CONT
2 CONT  might cut tile rope arid run off rrith Ihe sled, for it was rightfully
2 CONT
2 CONT  his, and he hatfn"t used it illegiillS' eui· lie clidr:'i dare. 19r. Kyg
2 CONT
2 CONT locked the sled in a shed and went his way. Ole sto~d there ill won-
2 CONT
2 CONT dermcnt. Then Iie went to the hoiise and kllockeJ. A dauyhte~ opcnEd
2 CONT
2 CONT the door and Ole said, "I want the sled that is in that buildiny."
2 CONT
2 CONT She dutifully opened the shed and gave it to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Some days went by. One night after Olf had gone to bed, his fa-
2 CONT
2 CONT ther came and awakened him. He had heard that Ole had the sled home
2 CONT
2 CONT without Ryg's permission. Ole pulled the bedclothes over his head, j
2 CONT
2 CONT but the father pulled them off again and said, "You are not too good
2 CONT
2 CONT to listen when you have set yourself against the authorities. Then
2 CONT
2 CONT they should have permission to take you." Ole had a bad conscience
2 CONT
2 CONT about it all and thought, "This will be something terrible."
2 CONT
2 CONT    After some time, Ole's father sent him to take Ryg to a place.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole thought, "Now he will say something." 6ut he didn't. They drove
2 CONT
2 CONT by a little pond with some beautiful waterlilifs growing in it. Ryg
2 CONT
2 CONT asked Ole to try to get some for him to give to his ~i:e. Ole sloshed
2 CONT
2 CONT into the water and got the flowers. It didn't matter if he got wetl
2 CONT
2 CONT Ryg paid Ole for the flowers, and said nothing about the sled. Fle
2 CONT
2 CONT was glad to get this off his conscience.
2 CONT
2 CONT    ihere were many trials and pain and sickness for Ole and Lovisa.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1918 Lovisa Iiad to go to Grand Forks for surgery. In 1923, Ole
2 CONT
2 CONT had digestive disorders that necessitated surgery also. Results of
2 CONT
2 CONT this were discomforts and pain. Several years later a doctor diag;
2 CONT
2 CONT nosed a case of ulcers, but surgery proved that this was net true,
2 CONT
2 CONT  and all through his later years he had ill effects of these surgeries.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Lovisa passed away in 1929. In 1932, Ole was married to Olga
2 CONT
2 CONT Fosserud. In 1947-45 Ole and Olga visited Thelma and Gordon Lovelady
2 CONT
2 CONT at Southgate, California. Other pleasant Ineinories include nu!.ierol;s
2 CONT
2 CONT trips by car in the community around tdinburg with brother Lars, Nikka,
2 CONT
2 CONT sister Karina Christopherson, and Ole and Olga. (In all his declining
2 CONT
2 CONT years Olga was his faithful companion and nurse until God took him
2 CONT
2 CONT home to Hiinself on Scpteniher 16, 1963.)
2 CONT
2 CONT     Farming was Ole's occupation, and he remembers everything about
2 CONT
2 CONT  this operation. Through the nearly 60 years of yearly croppings he
2 CONT
2 CONT  could relate the production per acre. Ole has rented part of his
2 CONT
2 CONT  land to his sons since 1936. Aiter 1941 they rented all of the land.
2 CONT
2 CONT  In that year he eioved into the front rooms of the house and lived
2 CONT
2 CONT  there until the fall of 1959. Tlien they all moved to Edinburg and
2 CONT
