1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Agnes Hartvikson, age 94, of Fargo, North Dakota, passed away Wednesday, October 12, 2011 at Sanford Health Palliative Care in Fargo, surrounded by her loving family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes B. Aarestad was born April 11, 1917, in Juanita, North Dakota in Foster County, to Torkel and Serina (Tengesdal) Aarestad.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She grew up in that area and graduated from school in McHenry, North Dakota.  Aggie continued her education in Grand Forks, North Dakota, graduating as a registered nurse from St. Michael’s School of Nursing.  After a courtship of
2 CONC  a few months, and a story worthy of any fairy tale, she married Gordon Hartvikson on November 29, 1943, in Little Rock, Arkansas.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1946, Aggie and Gordon made their home on a farm near Jamestown, North Dakota.  In addition to working on the family farm and raising their four children, Aggie worked at Jamestown Hospital as a pediatric nurse. Following a yea
2 CONC r in Perham, Minnesota, they moved together for the last time, making their home in North Fargo in 1974.  While there, Aggie worked for Eventide Lutheran Home, Moorhead, Minnesota, for ten years, retiring at the age of 70.  In Feb
2 CONC ruary of 1997, Aggie lost her loving husband of 54 years in a car accident.  After that, she was fortunate for the good health to live alone at Park Avenue Apartments in Fargo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her love for God was unwavering and was the foundation of her life.  All of her immediate family and many close family and friends loved and appreciated her God-fearing example.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aggie was the hub of all important family news and activities.  Family was precious to Aggie and she loved to spend time visiting and reminiscing.  Children and grandchildren alike would call her for the past and current events of
2 CONC  the family, which she would share with remarkable accuracy and recollection. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Over the final days of Aggie’s battle, the continual update to family and friends was that she had rallied yet again.  To all standing by it was a symbolic reminder of her life of constancy and endurance.  She was an example in li
2 CONC fe of how to live, laugh, and die to self, and ultimately how to die with dignity and peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aggie is survived by her children, Ron (Nancy) Hartvikson of Thompson, ND; Linda “Lyn” (Jim) Sorenson of De Queen, AR; Roberta “Byrd” (Lyle) Overby of Jamestown, ND; and Ann (Rick) Overby of West Fargo, ND; 14 grandchildren; 15 gr
2 CONC eat grandchildren; her brother, Wilfred (Thelma) Aarestad of Jamestown; her sister in law, Maxine Aarestad of Spring Valley, MN; and brother in law, Orval Sorenson of Alvord, IA.  A special recognition of the “only daughter that t
2 CONC urned out”, Elissa Erickson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aggie was preceded in death by her parents; her husband, Gordon; her brothers, Erv, Stan, and Sam; her sisters, Essie Boelter Tiemens and Mertis Sorenson; and grandson, Brent Sorenson.
