1 NAME Mylo Gene /Peterson/
2 GIVN Mylo Gene
2 _MARNM Peterson
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC North Dakota Veterans Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Mandan
3 STAE North Dakota
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 7 AUG 1947
2 ADDR
3 CITY Westhope
3 STAE North Dakota
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 30 NOV 2021
2 PLAC hospital
2 ADDR
3 CITY Mesa
3 STAE Arizona
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Burial record:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/234656625/mylo-gene-peterson
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Mylo Gene Peterson, 74, Mohall, passed away on Tuesday, November 30, 2021, at a Mesa, Arizona, hospital, surrounded by his family, just as the sun was beginning to set over the scenic mountains.  Burial at the North Dakota Veteran
2 CONC s Cemetery, rural Mandan, North Dakota.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mylo was born on August 7, 1947, in Westhope, North Dakota, to Ivan and Amanda (Tengesdal) Peterson.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He grew up on the family farm near Maxbass, North Dakota, and attended Renville Township School through the eighth grade and graduated from Mohall High School in 1965.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mylo attended NDSU, graduating with B.S. degrees in chemistry and math.  After college he served two years in the U.S Army at Ft. Lewis, Washington,  Ft. Sam Houston, Texas,  and Fitzsimmons Hospital in Denver, Colorado as a surgi
2 CONC cal tech, something he was very proud of and enjoyed sharing stories.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mylo met his wife, Dorothy, through a cousin, and they married on December 28, 1973.  They settled on the home farm near Maxbass, North Dakota before moving to Mohall, North Dakota in 1990.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mylo farmed his entire life with his brother, Galen, and eventually his two sons.  In 1975, he expanded the farming operation into the Redvers, Saskatchewan, area.  His greatest enjoyment in life was planting in the spring and har
2 CONC vesting in the fall.  He was extremely passionate about his profession, always researching and incorporating innovative agronomic and farming methods.  He and Galen were some of the first in the area to try no-till farming, a conc
2 CONC ept many considered crazy, but it is now the norm for the area.  Mylo’s favorite farming activity was swathing and he was very particular how it was done.  He passed his passion for agriculture on to his five children.
2 CONT
2 CONT He treasured time with his family and enjoyed taking them on vacations to many national park areas.  He and Dorothy took their dream trip in 2010, driving to Alaska in their camper van, exploring the beautiful sights.  They spent 
2 CONC winters at their home in Gold Canyon, Arizona, where they enjoyed the scenery, sunshine, wildlife and time with family.  Mylo was a great conversationalist and was always eager to visit with friends and family on anything from far
2 CONC ming to politics.  
2 CONT
2 CONT His family feels there wasn’t a question that he couldn’t answer.  He enjoyed watching NDSU Bison and Green Bay Packer football games.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mylo was a dedicated husband, father and grandfather who took the time to explain or teach things to his children and grandchildren.  He always made sure he got Dorothy gifts for important holidays and gave much thought in choosin
2 CONC g these, even if it meant taking one of his children shopping the day before Christmas to find the perfect gift.  Mylo will be remembered for his generosity, determination and intelligence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mylo was a member of Zion Lutheran Church and was a member of the George T. Taylor American Legion Post No. 52 in Mohall, North Dakota for 50 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family:  He is survived by his loving wife, Dorothy Peterson, Mohall, North Dakota;  children, Amy Peterson, Las Vegas, Nevada;  Sara (Loren) Bock, Belfield, North Dakota;  John (Stephanie Zelionka) Peterson, Redvers, Saskatchewan
2 CONC ;  Erica (Craig) Olson, Colfax, Saskatchewan;  Lucas (Sharla Hainsworth) Peterson, Redvers, Saskatchewasn.
2 CONT
2 CONT And grandchildren, Ava Bock, Carter, Andrew, Brynne and Paige Olson, Brooke and Owen Peterson, Kaiden and Jordyn Kerntopf.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mylo is also survived by siblings, Myrna Staton, Severn, Maryland; Myra (Lyle) Johnson, Bismarck, North Dakota; Galen Peterson, Maxbass, North Dakota;  brother-in-law, Dwayne (Gloria) Erickson, Georgetown, Texas;  sister-in-law, J
2 CONC ean (Mark) Thompson, Bismarck, North Dakota; and many nieces, nephews and cousins.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mylo was preceded in death by:  his parents, Ivan and Amanda Peterson, father- and mother-in-law, Morgan and Laura Erickson; brothers-in-law, Keith Fleck and David Staton.
2 CONT ----
